<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#papiamento">Glossary</a> · Term</p><h1 class="enc-title">Papiamento</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Papiamentu</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Papiamento is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/criollo-people">creole</a> language spoken on Aruba, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bonaire">Bonaire</a> and Curaçao in the southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caribbean">Caribbean</a>. Its vocabulary draws mainly on Portuguese and Spanish, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a>, English and African influences, and it developed among enslaved Africans and settlers from the 17th century. It is an official language of Aruba and Curaçao alongside Dutch.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/papiamento.jpg" alt="Papiamento" width="320" height="233" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: CROIX · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mapa_di_papiamentu.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Scholars dispute where Papiamento came from, and its precise history has not been established. Its parent language is West Iberian Romance, but some derive it from Portuguese and the Portuguese-based creoles, others from Spanish. Most linguists now believe it emerged from the Portuguese-based creoles of the West African coasts, since it shares many features with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cape-verdean-creole">Cape Verdean Creole</a> and Guinea-Bissau Creole. Bart Jacob has argued that it is a relexified offshoot of an early Upper Guinea Portuguese Creole carried to Curaçao from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/senegambia">Senegambia</a> in the second half of the 17th century, when the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> held the slave-trading island of Gorée. A local-development theory instead proposes a Portuguese-African pidgin formed in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caribbean">Caribbean</a>, with later Dutch and Spanish influences.</p><p>In 1634 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-west-india-company">Dutch West India Company</a> took possession of the islands, deporting most of the remaining Arawak and Spanish population and making them a hub of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> slave trade between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caribbean">Caribbean</a>. After 1654 the Jewish and Portuguese-speaking population of the islands grew substantially, as Portuguese-speaking Jews and their allies fled Northeast Brazil. The Sephardic community became the main merchants, and everyday trading was conducted in Papiamento. The language itself dates from about 1650.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>About 80 per cent of the vocabulary is Iberian in origin and about 20 per cent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a>, with some words from Native American and African languages. Many Iberian words cannot be assigned to Portuguese or Spanish, such as por fabor and señora. Words of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taino">Taíno</a> and Arawak origin, mostly for plants and animals, include maishi, corn, and kunuku, farm; African ones include pinda, peanut, from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-kongo">Kongo</a> mpinda. The grammar is typical of Atlantic creoles: time and aspect markers are used instead of tenses, there is no grammatical gender, and the plural is marked by the suffix -nan on definite nouns.</p><p>There are two main dialects, Papiamento on Aruba and Papiamentu on Curaçao and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bonaire">Bonaire</a>. Curaçao and Bonaire adopted the phonetic Römer-Maduro-Jonis spelling in 1976, and Aruba approved a more etymological one in 1977.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>Widespread use in official documents is first seen in the early 18th century. The oldest preserved texts are letters, the earliest of 1775. In the 19th century most materials on the islands were written in Papiamento, including Roman Catholic school books and hymnals. The Catecismo Corticu of 1837, the first printed book in the language, was added to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco">UNESCO</a>'s Memory of the World register in 2009, and the first newspaper, Civilisadó, appeared in 1871. Papiamento became an official language of Aruba in 2003 and of the Netherlands Antilles in 2007. The anthems of Aruba, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bonaire">Bonaire</a> and Curaçao are in it.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Papiamento” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Papiamento</b> and Dutch are official languages.</blockquote><a href="/state/aruba">Aruba</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Papiamentu</b> emerged as the common language.</blockquote><a href="/state/colony-of-curacao">Colony of Curaçao</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Papiamentu</b>, Dutch and English are official languages.</blockquote><a href="/state/curacao">Curaçao</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Papiamentu</b>, Dutch and English were spoken.</blockquote><a href="/state/netherlands-antilles">Netherlands Antilles</a></li></ul></section>
